\section{Ramon writes}

\paragraph{b)} Hay algo potentially confussing.

\paragraph{b.1)} La función ``contrasts'', en R, con valores como ``contr.treatment'' o ``contr.sum'' especifica cómo parametrizamos el modelo; o sea, cómo se construye la model.matrix. Interesante que el cambiar la patemetrización (el uso de \verb|options(contrasts = ...)|, o el mirar el setting vía \verb|options()$contrasts|) no aparece ni una sola vez en el documento de Law. Este uso de la función ``contrasts'' es el estándard y widespread, en R, y en la literatura as far as I remember (McCullagh \& Nelder, ``Generalized Linear Models'').

\paragraph{b.2)} Beyond that, we are interested in comparing (contrasting, testing) specific things. For example, something like ``all the experimental treatments vs.\ the Control'' or ``testing whether all pairs of means are equal'', or ``testing whether the `WT' is the average of `GoodMutation' and `BadMutation'\,''. Note that there is a possible ambiguity of ``treatment'' here, but here treatment are the labels in our factors.

The function makeContrasts in limma is used for this purpose you say ``I want to compare `WT' against the average of `GoodMutation' and `BadMutation'\,'' or ``Compare all treatments against the Control''. This returns the set of linear functions of the parameters so that you test what you want to test.

This is also what the function contrMat constructs in package multcomp. In fact, using Tukey contrasts in the multcomp package does exactly this: return the set of linear functions of the parameters so that you test what you want to test.

\paragraph{b.2)} Regardless of the setting of \verb|options()$contrasts| (e.g., whether ``contrasts.sum'' or ``contrasts.treatment'', or, if using package car, ``contrasts.Sum'', or ``contrasts.Treatment'', etc), you can always specify the comparisons you are interested in as a linear function of the parameters (the things shown under ``coefficients'') of your model. In other words, as linear functions of what is given by ``model.matrix''.

makeContrasts, in limma, makes this more transparent if you want, and you see directly what exactly you are comparing as a function of the model matrix.

contrMat in a sense hides it from you a little bit more (and, at the same time, seems more flexible).

Package emmeans uses yet another way of going around this in the sense that the function names and how one builds comparisons (aka contrasts or sets of hypothesis to test) of interest is somewhat different. (Explained in \url{https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/emmeans/vignettes/comparisons.html}) but the ideas are, of course, the same.

\paragraph{b.3)} Interestingly, the Law et al document does not discuss the settings of \verb|options()$contrasts|. The book by Dunn \& Smyth has a very brief section. And the documentation of contrMat does not dwell into it, and that of emmeans discusses it only a little.

It seems that, in particular for limma and makeContrasts, the implicit modus operandi of the authors is: `we will not mess around with changing \verb|options()$contrasts|; i.e., we won't do things like \verb|opt <- options(contrasts = c("contr.sum", "contr.poly"))|'. We just take things as R does them, and directly use the following route instead:

\begin{description}[leftmargin=3em,style=nextline]
\item[b.3.1:] we either use or not use an intercept (when, why, etc, is explained in the Law et al paper)
\item[b.3.2:] we use makeContrasts to compare what we care about
\end{description}

This is also what, to a large extent, ghlt does, and what ``Anova'' from the car package do.

In particular limma seems to say: R will parameterize the model in some way and we are just happy with it, and the only knob we turn is adding, or not, an intercept. That gives a particular model.matrix. Then, use makeContrasts.

I think the above is a reasonable summary of what is done and the sources of confusion. It would be good that it was explained.

\bigskip
\noindent\rule{\linewidth}{0.4pt}

I am still trying to figure out what ROS does here exactly. They use ``model.matrix'' exactly once in the book and \verb|options()$contrasts| (and realted) are not called even once. I need to look into that with more detail later. I think their logic is very different because, well, they are not asking for ``tests of hypothesis'' (no, no, we are Bayesians) but will eventually ask for posteriors of the things one cares about.

