\section{Claude responds and complements as}

\subsection*{(a) Why stop the reading of Law et al.\ at ``Study of interactions and additivity of treatments''}

Two reasons, one good and one accidental.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The good one:} through that section the paper covers what session~2 and the first hour of session~3 teach (one factor, contrasts, two factors with and without interaction). After it come nested factors, time courses, mixed effects and polynomial or cyclical covariates, which go beyond the block.
\item \textbf{The accidental one:} my read of the page was cut off shortly after, so I recommended within what I had seen.
\end{itemize}

I would now extend it by one subsection, ``Accounting for factors that are not of interest'' (batch effects), since \code{\textasciitilde{} batch + condition} is the design students will meet most. I have added it as optional.

In the part of the paper I could read, \code{options(contrasts = ...)}, \code{contr.sum}, \code{contr.treatment} and \code{contrasts()} are never named; the only knobs discussed are the default alphanumeric order of factor levels and \code{relevel} to change the reference level.

\subsection*{(b) The summary is right; two refinements}

The confusion is that R uses ``contrast'' for two different objects, and b.1 and b.2 separate them correctly. Here is a way to state it that I think resolves it for students.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The scientific question lives on the group means.} ``WT versus the average of the two mutants'' is a vector $\mathbf{c}$ applied to the vector of group (cell) means $\bm{\mu}$. It does not depend on any coding.
\item \textbf{The coding decides the model matrix.} \code{contr.treatment}, \code{contr.sum}, or dropping the intercept each give a different \code{model.matrix}, hence different coefficients $\bm{\beta}$ with different meanings, but the same fitted group means.
\item \textbf{The same question is a different vector under each coding.} Let $L$ be the matrix with one row of the model matrix per group, so that $\bm{\mu} = L\bm{\beta}$. Then
  \[
    \mathbf{c}^{\top}\bm{\mu} = (\mathbf{c}^{\top} L)\,\bm{\beta} = \mathbf{k}^{\top}\bm{\beta}.
  \]
  Change the coding and $L$, $\bm{\beta}$ and $\mathbf{k}$ change; the estimate of $\mathbf{c}^{\top}\bm{\mu}$, its standard error and its test do not.
\end{enumerate}

The tools differ only in which of these vectors you type:

\medskip
\noindent
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\raggedright}p{0.30\linewidth}
    >{\raggedright}X >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.27\linewidth}@{}}
  \toprule
  Tool & What you specify & Depends on the coding? \\
  \midrule
  \code{makeContrasts} (limma)
    & $\mathbf{k}$, in terms of the design matrix's column names
    & Yes; hence limma's preference for \code{\textasciitilde{} 0 + group} \\
  \code{contrMat} and \code{glht(..., mcp())} (multcomp)
    & $\mathbf{c}$, on the factor's level means; \code{mcp()} translates
      it to $\mathbf{k}$
    & No; the translation is done for you \\
  emmeans
    & $\mathbf{c}$, on estimated marginal means
    & No \\
  By hand
    & $\mathbf{k}$, on \code{coef(fit)}
    & Yes \\
  \bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\medskip

The refinements:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\code{contrMat} returns $\mathbf{c}$, not $\mathbf{k}$.} It builds contrasts among level means (Tukey, Dunnett). It is \code{glht} with \code{mcp()} that turns them into linear functions of the model's coefficients, using the model's own coding. So they become linear functions of the parameters only after that step.
\item \textbf{\code{car::Anova} is not quite in the same family.} Type~II tests are invariant to the coding. Type~III tests are not: they only mean what people expect under sum-to-zero coding. That is the one place in the course where \code{options(contrasts)} changes an answer, and it is already in \S20.6.1 and Appendix~B.3 of the notes.
\end{itemize}

The reading of limma in b.3 matches what I know: leave R's default coding alone, choose intercept or no intercept, then \code{makeContrasts}. The paper's own stated reason is that in a means model each parameter is a group mean, which makes comparisons simple to write as contrasts, while for a single treatment-versus-control comparison the mean-reference model is more direct, since its parameters already are the differences. One addition: \code{\textasciitilde{} 0 + a + b} only gives full indicator columns for the first factor, which is why the paper merges two factors into one before using a means model.

\subsection*{What ROS does}

I cannot check the book, but the guess above fits how I understand its logic, and it is the Bayesian version of the ``emmeans'' row of the table. You never need $\mathbf{k}$: take the posterior draws of the fitted means for the cells you care about (predictions at chosen inputs), apply $\mathbf{c}$ to each draw, and you have the posterior of the comparison. Coding never enters. Equivalently, multiply the matrix of coefficient draws by $\mathbf{k}$.

That gives a clean link for session~3: the ``WT versus average of mutants'' contrast computed by hand in session~2 becomes, with \code{stan\_glm}, a posterior distribution in two lines.

\subsection*{Implications for the course plan}

Session~2's second hour gets an explicit 10-minute step on the two meanings of ``contrast'', between the two parameterisations and the contrasts by hand, plus a note that the Law et al.\ paper will not make this point for the students.

I could not run R when writing this, so the invariance claim rests on the algebra, not on a run. It is worth a short demonstration in class with \code{contr.treatment} and \code{contr.sum} on the one-way example; the chunk below is an untested sketch of it, not evaluated.

<<invariance, eval=FALSE>>=
## UNTESTED SKETCH: the same comparison under two codings.
set.seed(1)
d <- data.frame(y = rnorm(30),
                g = factor(rep(c("WT", "GoodMutation", "BadMutation"),
                               each = 10)))
d$g <- relevel(d$g, ref = "WT")

f_t <- lm(y ~ g, data = d)  # default coding: contr.treatment
f_s <- lm(y ~ g, data = d, contrasts = list(g = "contr.sum"))

## L: one row of the model matrix per group
nd  <- data.frame(g = factor(levels(d$g), levels = levels(d$g)))
L_t <- model.matrix(delete.response(terms(f_t)), nd,
                    contrasts.arg = f_t$contrasts)
L_s <- model.matrix(delete.response(terms(f_s)), nd,
                    contrasts.arg = f_s$contrasts)

## c: WT versus the average of the two mutants, on the group means
cc <- c(WT = 1, GoodMutation = -0.5, BadMutation = -0.5)[levels(d$g)]

## k = c' L: different under each coding ...
k_t <- drop(cc %*% L_t)
k_s <- drop(cc %*% L_s)
rbind(k_t, k_s)

## ... but the same estimate and the same standard error
c(sum(k_t * coef(f_t)), sum(k_s * coef(f_s)))
c(sqrt(drop(t(k_t) %*% vcov(f_t) %*% k_t)),
  sqrt(drop(t(k_s) %*% vcov(f_s) %*% k_s)))
@

